1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,915
Most people use ChatGPT like a search box.

2
00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:04,240
They type a question, they get an answer,
and it's…

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,440
fine.

4
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:06,640
But here's the thing.

5
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,737
ChatGPT already knows dozens of ways to
think.

6
00:00:08,737 --> 00:00:09,937
It can compare.

7
00:00:09,937 --> 00:00:11,214
It can argue against itself.

8
00:00:11,214 --> 00:00:12,992
It can find risks you didn't ask about.

9
00:00:12,992 --> 00:00:15,181
It can break a problem down to first
principles.

10
00:00:15,181 --> 00:00:16,640
It just doesn't do any of that —

11
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:17,840
unless you ask.

12
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,624
In the next thirteen minutes I'm going to
show you a single block of text you paste

13
00:00:21,624 --> 00:00:22,824
into ChatGPT one time.

14
00:00:22,824 --> 00:00:26,653
After that, ChatGPT decides on its own
which of those thinking methods your question

15
00:00:26,653 --> 00:00:27,853
needs.

16
00:00:27,853 --> 00:00:29,053
No shortcuts.

17
00:00:29,053 --> 00:00:30,000
No slash commands.

18
00:00:30,253 --> 00:00:30,000
No memorising anything.

19
00:00:31,453 --> 00:00:30,000
You just talk normally —

20
00:00:32,653 --> 00:00:30,000
and the answers get sharper.

21
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:30,000
Let's build it.

22
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,679
First, let's be honest about what we're
building, because there's a lot of nonsense

23
00:00:35,679 --> 00:00:36,879
online.

24
00:00:36,879 --> 00:00:39,000
We are not installing software.

25
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,847
We are not buying a new AI.

26
00:00:40,847 --> 00:00:43,242
We are not unlocking a hidden mode.

27
00:00:43,242 --> 00:00:46,526
What we're doing is called a Custom
Instruction.

28
00:00:46,526 --> 00:00:48,032
Think of it like this.

29
00:00:48,032 --> 00:00:50,700
Imagine you hire a brilliant assistant.

30
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,137
Genuinely brilliant —

31
00:00:52,137 --> 00:00:53,337
knows everything.

32
00:00:53,337 --> 00:00:57,100
But on day one, they have no idea how you
like to work.

33
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:02,711
So every single morning you'd have to
repeat: "when I ask you something big, think

34
00:01:02,711 --> 00:01:08,321
it through properly, show me the risks,
tell me what you're assuming." Exhausting,

35
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:09,521
right?

36
00:01:09,521 --> 00:01:13,900
A Custom Instruction is a note you pin to
that assistant's desk.

37
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:15,100
Once.

38
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,384
They read it before every conversation,
forever.

39
00:01:18,384 --> 00:01:19,584
That's it.

40
00:01:19,584 --> 00:01:21,158
That's the whole trick.

41
00:01:21,158 --> 00:01:23,484
We are writing one very good note.

42
00:01:23,484 --> 00:01:27,111
I call this particular note the Universal
AI Engine —

43
00:01:27,111 --> 00:01:32,721
because instead of giving ChatGPT one
instruction, we're giving it a menu of about

44
00:01:32,721 --> 00:01:35,000
forty ways of thinking, plus the judgement
to pick the right ones by itself.

45
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,610
Now, you may have seen the popular version
of this idea online.

46
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:41,585
It usually looks like this.

47
00:01:41,585 --> 00:01:44,293
Someone gives you a list of commands.

48
00:01:44,293 --> 00:01:45,493
Slash-deep.

49
00:01:45,493 --> 00:01:46,693
Slash-godmode.

50
00:01:46,693 --> 00:01:47,893
Slash-critic.

51
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:49,093
Slash-simplify.

52
00:01:49,093 --> 00:01:55,020
And the deal is: if you remember to type
the magic word, you get a better answer.

53
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:58,166
I want you to notice the problem with
that.

54
00:01:58,166 --> 00:01:59,702
You have to remember.

55
00:01:59,702 --> 00:02:03,361
You have to know which method your
question needs.

56
00:02:03,361 --> 00:02:08,556
Which means you have to already understand
deep reasoning, red-teaming,

57
00:02:08,556 --> 00:02:12,434
first-principles thinking, and
second-order effects —

58
00:02:12,434 --> 00:02:14,776
just to pick the right shortcut.

59
00:02:14,776 --> 00:02:16,020
That's backwards.

60
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:18,361
You came here for help thinking.

61
00:02:18,361 --> 00:02:22,239
Now you need expertise just to ask for
help thinking.

62
00:02:22,239 --> 00:02:23,995
So this system flips it.

63
00:02:23,995 --> 00:02:30,141
Instead of "here are the commands you can
type", we tell ChatGPT: here is everything

64
00:02:30,141 --> 00:02:31,605
you know how to do —

65
00:02:31,605 --> 00:02:32,805
you choose.

66
00:02:32,805 --> 00:02:35,585
You type: "should I build this app?" —

67
00:02:35,585 --> 00:02:37,561
in completely normal words.

68
00:02:37,561 --> 00:02:43,415
And ChatGPT works out on its own that this
question deserves assumptions, risks,

69
00:02:43,415 --> 00:02:45,902
alternatives and a recommendation.

70
00:02:45,902 --> 00:02:47,102
Same question.

71
00:02:47,102 --> 00:02:48,302
No shortcut.

72
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:49,693
Much better answer.

73
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:50,000
That's the entire difference.

74
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,703
Let me show you what's happening
underneath, in plain language.

75
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:57,817
Every time you send a message, ChatGPT
doesn't just read your message.

76
00:02:57,817 --> 00:03:00,815
It reads three things stacked on top of
each other.

77
00:03:00,815 --> 00:03:02,015
Layer one —

78
00:03:02,015 --> 00:03:03,215
OpenAI's own rules.

79
00:03:03,215 --> 00:03:04,978
Safety, behaviour, the basics.

80
00:03:04,978 --> 00:03:07,565
You can't touch that, and you don't want
to.

81
00:03:07,565 --> 00:03:08,765
Layer two —

82
00:03:08,765 --> 00:03:10,234
your Custom Instructions.

83
00:03:10,234 --> 00:03:12,468
This is the layer we're editing today.

84
00:03:12,468 --> 00:03:13,996
It's your standing orders.

85
00:03:13,996 --> 00:03:18,169
It gets read before your message, every
single time, in every new chat.

86
00:03:18,169 --> 00:03:19,369
Layer three —

87
00:03:19,369 --> 00:03:20,956
the message you just typed.

88
00:03:20,956 --> 00:03:25,776
So when you write "should I build this
app?", ChatGPT is really seeing: "Reminder:

89
00:03:25,776 --> 00:03:30,419
you are a Universal AI Engine, here are
forty thinking methods, pick the useful

90
00:03:30,419 --> 00:03:34,181
ones, challenge assumptions, name risks,
end with a decision." —

91
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:35,533
and then your question.

92
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:37,237
Your question hasn't changed.

93
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:39,588
The instructions wrapped around it have.

94
00:03:39,588 --> 00:03:42,292
And that's why this feels like a different
AI.

95
00:03:42,292 --> 00:03:43,492
It isn't.

96
00:03:43,492 --> 00:03:47,371
It's the same model, finally being told
what good work looks like.

97
00:03:47,371 --> 00:03:52,191
One more thing, and this is important for
later: our instruction includes a recipe

98
00:03:52,191 --> 00:03:53,391
for hard questions.

99
00:03:53,391 --> 00:03:54,591
Understand —

100
00:03:54,591 --> 00:03:55,791
break it down —

101
00:03:55,791 --> 00:03:56,991
state assumptions —

102
00:03:56,991 --> 00:03:58,191
challenge them —

103
00:03:58,191 --> 00:03:59,484
look at alternatives —

104
00:03:59,484 --> 00:04:00,684
find risks —

105
00:04:00,684 --> 00:04:01,884
improve —

106
00:04:01,884 --> 00:04:03,084
decide —

107
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:04,284
give one next step.

108
00:04:04,284 --> 00:04:05,000
Nine steps.

109
00:04:05,484 --> 00:04:05,000
ChatGPT runs them quietly, in the
background, and hands you the finished thinking.

110
00:04:10,304 --> 00:04:05,000
Not the mess.

111
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,200
Okay.

112
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:07,954
Let's find the right screen.

113
00:04:07,954 --> 00:04:11,461
This is the part people get wrong, so go
slowly with me.

114
00:04:11,461 --> 00:04:13,653
We are not typing this into a chat.

115
00:04:13,653 --> 00:04:18,851
If you paste it into a normal
conversation, it only works in that one conversation,

116
00:04:18,851 --> 00:04:20,292
and it's gone tomorrow.

117
00:04:20,292 --> 00:04:23,924
We want it in Settings, so it applies
everywhere, forever.

118
00:04:23,924 --> 00:04:25,124
Here's the path.

119
00:04:25,124 --> 00:04:26,324
Open ChatGPT —

120
00:04:26,324 --> 00:04:28,767
the website or the app, either is fine.

121
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:31,711
Look for your profile picture or your
initials.

122
00:04:31,711 --> 00:04:34,780
On desktop it's usually bottom-left or
top-right.

123
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:38,162
On mobile, tap the menu, then your name at
the bottom.

124
00:04:38,162 --> 00:04:40,041
Click it, and choose Settings.

125
00:04:40,041 --> 00:04:41,669
Then find Personalization.

126
00:04:41,669 --> 00:04:45,552
Inside Personalization you're looking for
Custom instructions.

127
00:04:45,552 --> 00:04:46,752
Tap it.

128
00:04:46,752 --> 00:04:47,952
Now —

129
00:04:47,952 --> 00:04:49,152
one honest note.

130
00:04:49,152 --> 00:04:51,782
OpenAI redesigns this screen fairly often.

131
00:04:51,782 --> 00:04:55,853
The words might be slightly different by
the time you watch this.

132
00:04:55,853 --> 00:04:59,674
It may say "Personalization", it may say
"Customize ChatGPT".

133
00:04:59,674 --> 00:05:00,874
Don't panic.

134
00:05:00,874 --> 00:05:05,000
You are looking for the screen with the
empty boxes that ask what ChatGPT should

135
00:05:05,884 --> 00:05:05,000
know about you.

136
00:05:07,084 --> 00:05:05,000
That's always the right place.

137
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,200
You'll see a few boxes.

138
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,513
Typically: a nickname, what you do for
work, what traits ChatGPT should have, and a

139
00:05:10,513 --> 00:05:12,021
bigger one for anything else.

140
00:05:12,021 --> 00:05:13,221
Here's what matters.

141
00:05:13,221 --> 00:05:15,559
The box you want is the large free-text
one —

142
00:05:15,559 --> 00:05:19,873
usually the last one, labelled something
like "Anything else ChatGPT should know?",

143
00:05:19,873 --> 00:05:21,744
or on some versions, the traits box.

144
00:05:21,744 --> 00:05:22,944
Honestly?

145
00:05:22,944 --> 00:05:24,144
If you're unsure —

146
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:25,962
the big empty box is the right box.

147
00:05:25,962 --> 00:05:27,262
Both feed the same layer.

148
00:05:27,262 --> 00:05:28,613
Now paste the instruction.

149
00:05:28,613 --> 00:05:32,822
It's on screen, and it's in the
description below the video, so you never need to

150
00:05:32,822 --> 00:05:34,022
type it by hand.

151
00:05:34,022 --> 00:05:38,076
Let's read the key parts as it goes in, so
you actually understand what you're

152
00:05:38,076 --> 00:05:39,276
installing.

153
00:05:39,276 --> 00:05:41,615
Line one: "You are my Universal AI
Engine." —

154
00:05:41,615 --> 00:05:42,815
that sets the role.

155
00:05:42,815 --> 00:05:46,089
Then: "Automatically choose the best
methods for every request.

156
00:05:46,089 --> 00:05:48,064
I should not need to use shortcuts." —

157
00:05:48,064 --> 00:05:50,870
that's the whole point of the system,
stated up front.

158
00:05:50,870 --> 00:05:52,070
Then the long list —

159
00:05:52,070 --> 00:05:56,175
writing, simplifying, first principles,
Feynman, analogies, SWOT, steelman, red

160
00:05:56,175 --> 00:06:00,125
team, second-order effects, Pareto,
systems thinking, and about thirty more.

161
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:01,325
This is the menu.

162
00:06:01,325 --> 00:06:02,525
Then the guardrail —

163
00:06:02,525 --> 00:06:06,319
and this one matters more than people
think: "Select only what is useful.

164
00:06:06,319 --> 00:06:10,684
Do not overthink simple requests." Without
that line, you ask "what's the capital of

165
00:06:10,684 --> 00:06:13,127
France" and get a four-page strategic
analysis.

166
00:06:13,127 --> 00:06:15,102
With it, simple questions stay simple.

167
00:06:15,102 --> 00:06:17,596
Then the nine-step recipe for complex
decisions.

168
00:06:17,596 --> 00:06:18,796
Then the writing rule.

169
00:06:18,796 --> 00:06:19,996
Then the honesty rule —

170
00:06:19,996 --> 00:06:22,699
never invent facts, separate facts from
assumptions.

171
00:06:22,699 --> 00:06:26,544
And finally the language rule, which tells
it to answer in your language —

172
00:06:26,544 --> 00:06:29,299
even if you type that language using
English letters.

173
00:06:29,299 --> 00:06:30,499
Now —

174
00:06:30,499 --> 00:06:32,006
the two things people forget.

175
00:06:32,006 --> 00:06:33,669
One: make sure the toggle is on.

176
00:06:33,669 --> 00:06:37,203
Depending on your version it says "Enable
for new chats" or similar.

177
00:06:37,203 --> 00:06:38,918
If that's off, none of this runs.

178
00:06:38,918 --> 00:06:40,000
Two: press Save.

179
00:06:40,118 --> 00:06:40,000
Not back.

180
00:06:41,318 --> 00:06:40,000
Not escape.

181
00:06:42,518 --> 00:06:40,000
Save.

182
00:06:43,718 --> 00:06:40,000
If you navigate away without saving,
you've done nothing.

183
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,471
Now let's prove it's live.

184
00:06:41,471 --> 00:06:42,671
Two rules first.

185
00:06:42,671 --> 00:06:44,481
Rule one: open a brand new chat.

186
00:06:44,481 --> 00:06:48,157
Old conversations that were already
running may not pick this up.

187
00:06:48,157 --> 00:06:49,357
New chat.

188
00:06:49,357 --> 00:06:50,557
Always.

189
00:06:50,557 --> 00:06:53,215
Rule two: don't test it with something
trivial.

190
00:06:53,215 --> 00:06:54,460
Here's the test I use.

191
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:55,761
In a fresh chat, type —

192
00:06:55,761 --> 00:07:00,512
normally, no shortcuts: "Should I quit my
job to work on my side project full time?"

193
00:07:00,512 --> 00:07:01,756
Watch what comes back.

194
00:07:01,756 --> 00:07:03,849
You're looking for four fingerprints.

195
00:07:03,849 --> 00:07:05,049
One —

196
00:07:05,049 --> 00:07:09,743
it names the assumptions it's making,
instead of pretending to know your situation.

197
00:07:09,743 --> 00:07:10,943
Two —

198
00:07:10,943 --> 00:07:13,602
it gives you the case for and the case
against.

199
00:07:13,602 --> 00:07:14,802
Three —

200
00:07:14,802 --> 00:07:16,951
it raises risks you did not ask about.

201
00:07:16,951 --> 00:07:18,151
Four —

202
00:07:18,151 --> 00:07:21,714
it ends with an actual recommendation and
a concrete next step.

203
00:07:21,714 --> 00:07:23,072
Not "it depends on you".

204
00:07:23,072 --> 00:07:25,504
If you see those four things, it's
working.

205
00:07:25,504 --> 00:07:29,633
If you see a flat, generic, five-bullet
answer with no assumptions and no

206
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:30,000
recommendation —

207
00:07:30,833 --> 00:07:30,000
go back to Settings.

208
00:07:32,033 --> 00:07:30,000
Nine times out of ten, the toggle is off,
or you never hit Save.

209
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,223
Let's watch it work on real questions.

210
00:07:32,223 --> 00:07:33,423
Three examples.

211
00:07:33,423 --> 00:07:34,623
Watch what I type —

212
00:07:34,623 --> 00:07:37,665
because I'm going to type like a normal
human being.

213
00:07:37,665 --> 00:07:38,865
Example one.

214
00:07:38,865 --> 00:07:42,785
I type: "I want to build an app that
reminds people to drink water.

215
00:07:42,785 --> 00:07:44,540
Is it a good idea?" That's it.

216
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:45,740
Eleven words.

217
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,905
No commands, no formatting, no magic.

218
00:07:47,905 --> 00:07:49,426
Now watch what comes back.

219
00:07:49,426 --> 00:07:51,181
It doesn't just say yes or no.

220
00:07:51,181 --> 00:07:54,574
First it separates what it knows from what
it's assuming —

221
00:07:54,574 --> 00:07:59,195
it flags that it doesn't know my budget,
my audience, or whether I can build it

222
00:07:59,195 --> 00:08:00,395
myself.

223
00:08:00,395 --> 00:08:05,310
Then it does something I never asked for:
it compares my idea to what already exists

224
00:08:05,310 --> 00:08:06,510
—

225
00:08:06,510 --> 00:08:11,307
and points out that phones already have
reminders built in, and that this category

226
00:08:11,307 --> 00:08:12,535
is extremely crowded.

227
00:08:12,535 --> 00:08:13,735
Then risks.

228
00:08:13,735 --> 00:08:14,935
Retention.

229
00:08:14,935 --> 00:08:16,983
People delete these apps in a week.

230
00:08:16,983 --> 00:08:19,616
That's the killer, and I didn't ask about
it.

231
00:08:19,616 --> 00:08:20,816
Then it reframes —

232
00:08:20,816 --> 00:08:25,613
it suggests the real opportunity might not
be reminders at all, but a narrow group

233
00:08:25,613 --> 00:08:27,836
with a medical reason to track intake.

234
00:08:27,836 --> 00:08:29,036
And then it decides.

235
00:08:29,036 --> 00:08:33,950
A recommendation, and one next step: talk
to ten people in that group before writing

236
00:08:33,950 --> 00:08:35,150
a line of code.

237
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:36,350
Now —

238
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:37,871
notice what it did not do.

239
00:08:37,871 --> 00:08:41,849
It never said "I'm now applying
comparison, then risk analysis, then

240
00:08:41,849 --> 00:08:43,370
decision-making." It just…

241
00:08:43,370 --> 00:08:44,570
thought properly.

242
00:08:44,570 --> 00:08:45,000
That's deliberate.

243
00:08:45,770 --> 00:08:45,000
The last line of our instruction says the
goal is the best answer, not showing off

244
00:08:50,568 --> 00:08:45,000
the method.

245
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,200
Example two.

246
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:48,269
A real decision with real trade-offs.

247
00:08:48,269 --> 00:08:51,625
I type: "Should I build a SaaS or a mobile
app?" Nine words.

248
00:08:51,625 --> 00:08:52,825
Deliberately vague —

249
00:08:52,825 --> 00:08:55,006
because that's how people actually ask.

250
00:08:55,006 --> 00:08:56,684
Watch the shape of the answer.

251
00:08:56,684 --> 00:09:01,270
First it does the thing almost nobody
does: it says the question can't be answered

252
00:09:01,270 --> 00:09:05,409
as asked, and tells you exactly which
three facts would change the answer.

253
00:09:05,409 --> 00:09:06,609
Who's paying.

254
00:09:06,609 --> 00:09:08,455
Whether it needs to work offline.

255
00:09:08,455 --> 00:09:11,754
And whether you're selling to businesses
or to individuals.

256
00:09:11,754 --> 00:09:12,954
But —

257
00:09:12,954 --> 00:09:14,154
and this is the key —

258
00:09:14,154 --> 00:09:16,224
it does not stop and interrogate you.

259
00:09:16,224 --> 00:09:19,244
It states its assumptions out loud and
answers anyway.

260
00:09:19,244 --> 00:09:22,991
That's the difference between a useful
advisor and an annoying one.

261
00:09:22,991 --> 00:09:24,445
Then a genuine comparison.

262
00:09:24,445 --> 00:09:25,645
Cost to build.

263
00:09:25,645 --> 00:09:27,323
Time to first paying customer.

264
00:09:27,323 --> 00:09:28,523
App-store approval —

265
00:09:28,523 --> 00:09:30,704
which can take days and can reject you.

266
00:09:30,704 --> 00:09:34,619
Ongoing maintenance across two phone
platforms instead of one website.

267
00:09:34,619 --> 00:09:36,353
Then the second-order effects —

268
00:09:36,353 --> 00:09:38,422
the consequences of the consequences.

269
00:09:38,422 --> 00:09:43,064
Choose mobile, and you've signed up for
app-store fees and update cycles for years.

270
00:09:43,064 --> 00:09:46,140
That's not a launch problem, it's a
three-year problem.

271
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:47,986
Most people don't think that far.

272
00:09:47,986 --> 00:09:52,684
Then a clear recommendation, with the
exact condition that would flip it: "go SaaS —

273
00:09:52,684 --> 00:09:57,214
unless your product genuinely needs the
camera, GPS, or push notifications." That

274
00:09:57,214 --> 00:09:59,116
last part is what makes it usable.

275
00:09:59,116 --> 00:10:00,000
It doesn't just decide.

276
00:10:00,402 --> 00:10:00,000
It tells you when to change your mind.

277
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:01,200
Third example —

278
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,564
and this one shows the system going the
other way.

279
00:10:03,564 --> 00:10:07,205
I type: "What is an AI Agent?" Now,
remember the rule: don't overthink simple

280
00:10:07,205 --> 00:10:08,405
requests.

281
00:10:08,405 --> 00:10:09,776
This is a knowledge question.

282
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:11,951
There's no decision to make, no risk to
weigh.

283
00:10:11,951 --> 00:10:15,166
So a good engine should not run the
nine-step decision process here.

284
00:10:15,166 --> 00:10:16,366
And it doesn't.

285
00:10:16,366 --> 00:10:18,210
Instead it switches into teaching mode.

286
00:10:18,210 --> 00:10:19,410
Watch.

287
00:10:19,410 --> 00:10:23,003
It starts with the plainest possible
sentence: a normal chatbot answers you.

288
00:10:23,003 --> 00:10:24,895
An agent actually goes and does things —

289
00:10:24,895 --> 00:10:27,731
several steps, in order, using tools,
until the job is done.

290
00:10:27,731 --> 00:10:28,931
Then an analogy —

291
00:10:28,931 --> 00:10:31,532
that's the Feynman method, explaining like
you're five.

292
00:10:31,532 --> 00:10:33,943
A chatbot is a colleague who answers your
question.

293
00:10:33,943 --> 00:10:37,726
An agent is a colleague who takes the
task, disappears, and comes back when it's

294
00:10:37,726 --> 00:10:38,926
finished.

295
00:10:38,926 --> 00:10:40,126
Then a concrete example.

296
00:10:40,126 --> 00:10:44,003
"Find me a flight" versus "book me a
flight, compare four sites, pick the cheapest

297
00:10:44,003 --> 00:10:47,313
with legroom, and put it in my calendar."
Then the honest limitation —

298
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:51,190
agents break in ways chatbots don't,
because one wrong step early ruins every step

299
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:52,390
after it.

300
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:54,423
And it ends by checking your
understanding.

301
00:10:54,423 --> 00:10:55,623
Same instruction block.

302
00:10:55,623 --> 00:10:57,088
Completely different behaviour.

303
00:10:57,088 --> 00:11:00,000
Because the engine read the question and
picked teaching, analogies and examples —

304
00:11:00,965 --> 00:11:00,000
instead of risks and decisions.

305
00:11:02,431 --> 00:11:00,000
That is the whole promise of this system,
working.

306
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,954
Four things that make this dramatically
better.

307
00:11:02,954 --> 00:11:04,154
One —

308
00:11:04,154 --> 00:11:05,411
write like you talk.

309
00:11:05,411 --> 00:11:08,051
Don't try to write clever prompts anymore.

310
00:11:08,051 --> 00:11:10,440
The instruction is doing that job now.

311
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:15,720
Messy, honest, human questions actually
work best, because the more context you give

312
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:17,354
it, the better it reasons.

313
00:11:17,354 --> 00:11:18,554
Two —

314
00:11:18,554 --> 00:11:21,069
always start a new chat for a new topic.

315
00:11:21,069 --> 00:11:23,269
The instruction loads at the start.

316
00:11:23,269 --> 00:11:26,977
Don't drag a two-week-old conversation
into a new decision.

317
00:11:26,977 --> 00:11:28,177
Three —

318
00:11:28,177 --> 00:11:29,811
you can still override it.

319
00:11:29,811 --> 00:11:32,389
The instruction is a default, not a cage.

320
00:11:32,389 --> 00:11:34,903
If it goes too deep, just say "shorter".

321
00:11:34,903 --> 00:11:38,171
If it goes too shallow, say "challenge
that harder".

322
00:11:38,171 --> 00:11:41,880
It obeys the message in front of it over
the standing note.

323
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:43,080
Four —

324
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:44,903
add two lines about yourself.

325
00:11:44,903 --> 00:11:47,920
Your job, your language, what you're
working on.

326
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:53,137
Because "should I build this app?" is a
completely different question for a student

327
00:11:53,137 --> 00:11:55,000
and for someone with a team of ten.

328
00:11:55,337 --> 00:11:55,000
Give it that, and the quality jumps
immediately.

329
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,540
Now the part most tutorials skip.

330
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:58,126
Let's be straight with each other.

331
00:11:58,126 --> 00:11:59,386
This is not a new AI model.

332
00:11:59,386 --> 00:12:00,586
It's the same ChatGPT.

333
00:12:00,586 --> 00:12:02,032
You have not upgraded anything.

334
00:12:02,032 --> 00:12:03,572
You've given better instructions.

335
00:12:03,572 --> 00:12:04,772
This is not software.

336
00:12:04,772 --> 00:12:05,972
Nothing was installed.

337
00:12:05,972 --> 00:12:07,745
There's no app, no plugin, no account.

338
00:12:07,745 --> 00:12:09,051
It's text in a settings box.

339
00:12:09,051 --> 00:12:10,544
It doesn't make ChatGPT correct.

340
00:12:10,544 --> 00:12:12,130
It makes it think more carefully —

341
00:12:12,130 --> 00:12:14,603
and a careful thinker can still be
confidently wrong.

342
00:12:14,603 --> 00:12:18,009
Our instruction tells it never to invent
facts and to separate facts from

343
00:12:18,009 --> 00:12:19,782
assumptions, and that genuinely helps.

344
00:12:19,782 --> 00:12:21,275
It does not make it a guarantee.

345
00:12:21,275 --> 00:12:22,581
Check anything that matters.

346
00:12:22,581 --> 00:12:25,474
And here's the most important distinction
in this whole video.

347
00:12:25,474 --> 00:12:27,853
Custom Instructions are guaranteed to be
delivered.

348
00:12:27,853 --> 00:12:29,053
That part is mechanical —

349
00:12:29,053 --> 00:12:32,413
OpenAI reliably puts your text in front of
the model, in every new chat.

350
00:12:32,413 --> 00:12:33,952
That's the part you can count on.

351
00:12:33,952 --> 00:12:36,285
Whether the model follows every line,
every time —

352
00:12:36,285 --> 00:12:37,485
that's not a guarantee.

353
00:12:37,485 --> 00:12:39,771
That's a language model doing its best to
comply.

354
00:12:39,771 --> 00:12:43,551
It'll follow the spirit almost always, and
occasionally drift on a specific rule.

355
00:12:43,551 --> 00:12:44,751
So: delivery, guaranteed.

356
00:12:44,751 --> 00:12:45,951
Obedience, very likely.

357
00:12:45,951 --> 00:12:47,151
Not certain.

358
00:12:47,151 --> 00:12:49,483
Anyone telling you otherwise is selling
something.

359
00:12:49,483 --> 00:12:51,723
Last one: it applies to new chats, not old
ones.

360
00:12:51,723 --> 00:12:55,000
And if you use ChatGPT on your phone and
your laptop, it follows your account —

361
00:12:55,409 --> 00:12:55,000
so it's already on both.

362
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,500
Let's put it together.

363
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:02,227
You opened Settings, went to
Personalization, opened Custom instructions, pasted one

364
00:13:02,227 --> 00:13:06,386
block of text, made sure the toggle was
on, and pressed Save.

365
00:13:06,386 --> 00:13:08,500
That's the entire installation.

366
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:10,477
Maybe ninety seconds of work.

367
00:13:10,477 --> 00:13:16,068
And from now on, in every new chat,
ChatGPT reads a note that says: here are forty

368
00:13:16,068 --> 00:13:17,295
ways of thinking —

369
00:13:17,295 --> 00:13:20,500
you work out which ones this question
deserves.

370
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:22,205
You don't type shortcuts.

371
00:13:22,205 --> 00:13:24,114
You don't memorise commands.

372
00:13:24,114 --> 00:13:25,545
You ask like a human.

373
00:13:25,545 --> 00:13:28,136
The instruction is in the description.

374
00:13:28,136 --> 00:13:33,864
Paste it, change the language line to your
own language, and go and ask it something

375
00:13:33,864 --> 00:13:35,636
you're genuinely stuck on.

376
00:13:35,636 --> 00:13:38,364
That's where you'll feel the difference.

377
00:13:38,364 --> 00:13:40,000
See you in the next one.

